## Context

Tracker 0760 cannot yet demonstrate its fourth exit criterion. The repository
has one prepared real archive, `2026-09`; the current `2026-10` comparison is
deliberately synthetic and has no immutable package, descriptor, routes, or
downloads to replay. The next rehearsal must demonstrate the release mechanics
without fabricating a changed JETP fact or publishing anything.

Parent: 0760 / GitHub #1353. Reuse the prepared 2026-09 package and the existing
release/bundle utilities from 0726--0728. This ticket authorizes no source
collection, data expansion, publication, deployment, estimator, MVP v0.2 work,
or short-paper work.

## First test

Starting from the accepted `2026-09` archive, build a second reviewed rehearsal
edition from the same frozen source facts. Restore both archives into separate
offline directories, then assert each restored site contains all four country
JSON downloads, the overview/comparison handoffs, and the stable routes listed
by its manifest.

Run two independent failure controls against the second edition: (1) interrupt
the builder while it writes the candidate; (2) let the builder finish, then
reject the candidate during validation. For **each** control, assert that the
`2026-09` descriptor and archive retain their exact pre-failure SHA-256 and
bytes, then restore and replay its routes/downloads offline again. The two
controls must not share a single assertion that could pass after only one path.

The RED must fail if a second descriptor/archive is absent, if either restoration
needs Git, DVC, network access, or source documents, or if either candidate
failure path can alter or prevent offline replay of the first archive.

## Scope

Create two immutable static release packages and descriptors with distinct
edition identities and explicit prepared/rehearsal state. The second package may
reuse unchanged facts and downloads, but must record its exact input Git revision,
cutoff, predecessor identity, and a transparent no-scientific-change result. It
must not relabel an unchanged observation as a new event or manufacture a
correction merely to populate the second edition.

Extend the release-history and offline-replay checks only as needed to prove that
both packages restore independently and preserve routes/downloads. Retain the
first archive byte-for-byte after an interrupted or rejected candidate. Document
the rehearsal, named release reviewer, unresolved coverage, and the distinction
between prepared local packages and a public deployment.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Two distinct immutable release descriptors and self-contained static
      packages exist, with a documented relationship and no invented scientific
      change.
- [ ] Each package restores offline without Git, DVC, network, or raw source
      documents, and exposes four-country downloads plus manifest-listed routes.
- [ ] An interrupted second-edition builder leaves the first descriptor/archive
      byte-identical by SHA-256 and bytes, and its offline routes/downloads
      replay after the interruption.
- [ ] A completed but validation-rejected second-edition candidate independently
      leaves the first descriptor/archive byte-identical by SHA-256 and bytes,
      and its offline routes/downloads replay after rejection.
- [ ] Release history presents both editions with accurate state, cutoff, and
      preparation metadata; prior download references remain available.
- [ ] No canonical JETP facts, source archive, candidate ownership, MVP public
      claims, deployment state, research protocol, MVP v0.2, or paper result is
      added or altered.

## Validation and recovery

Begin with the failing two-archive replay fixture. Run focused public-release,
bundle, monthly-edition, and observatory-route tests; run `make check-fast` and
`make lint`. This touches pipeline/release code, so run the full `make check`
under AGENTS.md and qualify unavailable environment inputs honestly. Run the
existing browser exercise if renderer output changes. Cite descriptor/archive
SHA-256 values and the exact input revision in the PR. Preserve the frozen MVP
baseline and unrelated workspace files. No deployment or external publication.

